%I A401784 #10 Nov 06 2026 04:23:04
%S A401784 4,12,20,28,44,52,60,68,76,84,92,116,124,132,140,144,148,156,164,172,
%T A401784 188,200,204,212,220,228,236,244,260,268,276,284,292,308,316,332,340,
%U A401784 348,356,364,372,380,388,404,412,420,428,436,444,452,460,476,492,508,516
%N A401784 Numbers k such that A322857(k) = 3*k/2.
%C A401784 First differs from its subsequence A081770 at n = 16: a(16) = 144 is not a term in A081770.
%C A401784 The relative asymptotic density of A081770 within this sequence is 1/(Pi^2*d) = 0.963602..., where d is the asymptotic density of this sequence that is mentioned below.
%C A401784 First differs from A401189 at n = 21: a(16) = 144 is not a term in A401189. Differs from A401189 by having the terms 144, 200, 600, 720, 1008, ..., and not having the terms 648, 3240, 4536, 5184, 5308, ... .
%C A401784 First differs from A401751 at n = 16: a(16) = 144 is not a term in A401751.
%C A401784 Differs from A364999 by having the terms 4, 144, 200, 600, 720, ... .
%C A401784 Equivalently, numbers k such that the sum of exponential unitary divisors of k that are smaller than k equals k/2.
%C A401784 All the terms are even by definition.
%C A401784 All the terms are nonsquarefree (A013929), since A322857(k) = k for squarefree numbers k.
%C A401784 Analogous to e-unitary perfect numbers (A054979 \ A322858), which have a ratio A322857(k)/k = 2 instead of 3/2.
%C A401784 Analogous to hemiperfect numbers (A159907) with the sum of exponential unitary divisors (A322857) instead of the sum of divisors (A000203). Are there numbers k with half-integer values of A322857(k)/k other than 3/2? There are none below 10^20.
%C A401784 If m is a term, and k is a squarefree number coprime to m, then k*m is also a term.
%C A401784 The primitive terms in this sequence are the powerful terms (A401785). This sequence can be generated from the primitive terms by multiplying with coprime squarefree numbers.
%C A401784 The asymptotic density of this sequence is Sum_{n>=1} f(A401785(n)) = 0.105148301674860659..., where  f(n) = (6/(Pi^2*n)) * Product_{prime p|n} (p/(p+1)).
%H A401784 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A401784/b401784.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%t A401784 f[p_, e_] := DivisorSum[e, p^# &, CoprimeQ[#, e/#] &]; eusigma[1] = 1; eusigma[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; q[k_] := eusigma[k] == 3*k/2; Select[2 * Range[258], q]
%o A401784 (PARI) isok(k) = if(k%2, 0, vecprod([sumdiv(f[2], d, if(gcd(f[2]/d, d) == 1, f[1]^d)) | f <- factor(k)~]) == 3*k/2);
%Y A401784 Subsequence of A005843 and A013929.
%Y A401784 Subsequences: A081770, A401785.
%Y A401784 Cf. A000203, A005117, A054979, A159907, A322857, A322858, A361255, A401751.
%Y A401784 Cf. A364999, A401189.
%K A401784 nonn,easy,new
%O A401784 1,1
